      ":question/id": "66a4bf4d-3ef4-5f49-a352-4ec58bcd4ae7",
      ":question/problem": "Consider the following system of equations.\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - 10y &=23 \\\\ -2x - y &=-20\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nDescribe the steps needed to eliminate the variable $y$ from the system:\nUse an integer multiplier and leave the other equation unchanged.\n\nTo eliminate $y$, multiply the\n{{field-1}}\nequation by\n{{field-2}},\nand add the equations.",
      ":question/worked-solution": "Notice that the coefficient of $y$ in the first equation is $-10$, while in the second equation it is $-1$.\n\nIf we multiply the second equation by ${\\color{blue}-10}$, then the coefficient of $y$ will turn into $10$, which will cancel with the coefficient of $-10$ in the first equation.\n\n$$\n\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}&-3x - 10y = 23 \\\\ &(-2x - y = -20)\\cdot (-10)\\end{aligned}\\right.\\quad\\Longrightarrow\\quad\\left\\{\\begin{aligned}-3x - 10y &=23 \\\\ 20x + 10y &=200\\end{aligned}\\right.\n$$\n\nNow, when we add the two equations, the variable $y$ will be eliminated:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\n-3x - \\cancel{10y} &= 23 \\\\\n20x + \\cancel{10y} &= 200 \\\\\n(-3x + 20x) &= (23 + 200) \\\\\n17x &= 223\n\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is the following:\n\n> *To eliminate $y$, multiply the $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}\\text{second}}$ equation by $\\bbox[3pt,Gainsboro]{\\color{blue}-10}$, and add the equations*.",
